ReXGlue codegen: find the index register of an indexed jump-table load in either operand order.

detectJumpTable() took rB of lwzx/lbzx/lhzx rD, rA, rB as the index. Compilers can also emit
the table in rB and the scaled index in rA, as in 007: Quantum of Solace's sub_821C1B90:

    lis    r11, table@ha
    rlwinm r10, r3, 2, 0, 29      ; index * 4
    addi   r9, r11, table@l       ; table address
    lwzx   r8, r10, r9            ; rA = index, rB = table
    mtctr  r8
    bctr

The generated switch then tested the table address (r9) instead of the index (r3), always fell
to the default case and hit __builtin_trap() (illegal instruction, 0xC000001D). Both operands
are now traced back; the one built by lis/addi/ori is the table, the other the index. Tables in
the usual order (table in rA) come out as before.

